<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church Fathers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Early Church Fathers, Fathers of the Church, Christian Fathers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church Fathers were ancient Christian theologians and writers who laid the intellectual and doctrinal foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Their age, the Patristic Era, ran roughly from the late 1st to the mid-8th century and flourished in the 4th and 5th centuries, as Christianity became the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. The earliest, the Apostolic Fathers such as Clement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Ignatius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and Polycarp of Smyrna, were believed to have known or been shaped by the apostles. Many denominations count their writings as part of Sacred Tradition, though some, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tertullian">Tertullian</a>, had parts of their teaching later condemned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-fathers.jpg" alt="Church Fathers" width="330" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otsy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Fathers are usually grouped by language and region. The Apostolic Fathers, of whom Clement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Ignatius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and Polycarp of Smyrna are counted the chief, were believed to have known some of the Twelve Apostles or to have been shaped by them. Their writings were popular in early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> but were not included in the canon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> once it took its final form. Ignatius, the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Antioch, wrote a series of letters on his way to martyrdom in Rome, dealing with the sacraments, the role of bishops and the incarnation of Christ.</p><p>Those who wrote in Greek are the Greek Fathers: Justin Martyr, Irenaeus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-of-alexandria">Clement of Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasius-of-alexandria">Athanasius of Alexandria</a>, the Cappadocians Basil of Caesarea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nazianzus">Gregory of Nazianzus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nyssa">Gregory of Nyssa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a>, Cyril of Alexandria and John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> among them. In the Catholic tradition four Latin Fathers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a>, are called the Great Church Fathers. A smaller group wrote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, among them Aphrahat, who worked within the Persian Empire, and Ephrem the Syrian. The Desert Fathers, early monastics in the Egyptian desert such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-the-great">Anthony the Great</a> and Pachomius, wrote less, but their sayings were gathered in the Apophthegmata Patrum and they are credited with forming the basis of monasticism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Churches differ on where the age of the Fathers ends. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> the patristic era is believed to have passed, with John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> among those considered the last Fathers, though the Church grants the similar title of Doctor of the Church to later saints. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> does not consider the age over and includes influential writers up to the present day. Its doctrine rests on the consensus of the Holy Fathers, the points on which they agree, rather than on any one of them being infallible.</p><p>The Lutheran Augsburg Confession of 1530 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/formula-of-concord">Formula of Concord</a> of 1576 to 1584 both begin with the doctrine professed by the Fathers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a>, and the first Protestant reformers, like the Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches, used the Fathers' interpretations of scripture. The academic study of the Fathers is called patristics. Their works before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> were translated into English in the nineteenth-century collection Ante-Nicene Fathers, and later ones in Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Church Fathers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The writers of the early church, the <b>Church Fathers</b>, shaped its doctrine: Irenaeus, Origen and Tertullian in the 2nd and 3rd centuries; Athanasius, the Cappadocian Fathers, John Chrysostom, Ambrose, Jerome and Augustine of Hippo in the 4th and 5th.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Until 1945 Gnosticism was known mainly from the <b>Church Fathers</b> who opposed it, and their writings had an antagonistic bias and were incomplete.</blockquote><a href="/religion/gnosticism">Gnosticism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 2nd century CE the <b>Church Fathers</b> condemned the empire’s non-Christian religions as “pagan”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Biographers, novelists, satirists and historians, such as Plutarch, Lucian and Polybius, continued the tradition, and the New Testament and the works of the <b>Church Fathers</b> made Greek the language of Christian thought.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>There were sermons and saints’ lives, biblical translations, the works of the early <b>Church Fathers</b>, laws and wills, and books on grammar, medicine and geography.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Humanist scholars called for a return to the sources of Christianity, the Bible in its original languages and the early <b>Church Fathers</b>; in 1516 Erasmus published the Greek New Testament with notes correcting the Latin Vulgate.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li></ul></section>
